Project rental_star_schema {
  database_type: 'PostgreSQL'
  Note: '''
    Grain: one row in fact_rental represents one rental of one inventory copy of a film by one customer.
    Row count to match in Week 3: 51,805 rentals.
    Open rentals: 241 have no return date. days_kept is empty for them, and return_date_key points to the Not returned yet row in dim_date.
    Payments are not in this fact table. 749 rentals have no payment, and rental 4591 has six, five of them under other customers. Payments get their own fact table, one row per payment.
    Categories: a film can have several, so category totals add up to more than total rentals.
    Slowly changing dimensions: dim_customer, dim_film, dim_store and dim_staff keep history with Type 2 rows. Each has valid_from, valid_to and is_current. The current row has valid_to 9999-12-31. The first version of each row has valid_from 1900-01-01, so rentals from before the first load still find a version.
    Type 4 was considered and not used. Mini-dimensions target very large dimensions with fast-changing attributes, and these dimensions are small with slow changes. A separate history table would force a date-range join into every report, while Type 2 stores the right version key in the fact table once, when the row is loaded.
    Data quality rule 1: the customer on a payment must match the customer on its rental. Rental 4591 breaks it.
    Data quality rule 2: the date of a payment must not be earlier than the date of its rental. 14,665 of 51,061 payments break it, confirmed by query on the database.
    Row counts on the first load, confirmed by query on the database: 999 customers, 1,000 films, 500 stores, 1,500 staff, 16 categories, 2,367 film-category pairs. Type 2 adds rows over time.
  '''
}

Table fact_rental {
  rental_id int [pk]
  inventory_id int
  customer_key int
  store_key int
  film_key int
  staff_key int
  return_date_key int
  rental_date_key int
  rental_count int
  days_kept int
  Note: '''
    Grain: one rental of one inventory copy of a film by one customer. Must have 51,805 rows.
    Each dimension key points to the version of that dimension row that was true on the rental date.
    days_kept is the return date minus the rental date in whole days. It is additive, and empty for the 241 open rentals.
    rental_count is always 1.
    store_key is the store that owns the copy, found through inventory.
    Payment amounts are not stored here.
  '''
}

Table dim_date {
  date_key int [pk]
  full_date date
  day_of_week_number int
  day_name text
  day_of_month int
  month_number int
  month_name text
  calendar_year int
  quarter int
  is_weekend boolean
  Note: '''
    date_key is a readable integer such as 20220524. One row per day, including days with no rentals.
    Special row with key -1, labelled Not returned yet, for the 241 open rentals.
    Used twice by fact_rental: for the rental date and the return date.
  '''
}

Table bridge_film_category {
  film_key int
  category_key int
  indexes {
    (film_key, category_key) [pk]
  }
  Note: 'Many-to-many link between films and categories. 2,367 rows. Built in Week 3.'
}

Table dim_category {
  category_key int [pk]
  category_id int
  category_name text
  Note: '16 rows.'
}

Table dim_staff {
  staff_key int [pk]
  staff_id int
  first_name text
  last_name text
  email text
  is_active boolean
  valid_from date
  valid_to date
  is_current boolean
  Note: '''
    1,500 rows on the first load. Rentals only use 2 of the 1,500 staff, confirmed by query on the database.
    Type 2: is_active, so a rental shows whether the staff member was active when they handled it.
    Type 1: email and names. A Type 1 change overwrites every version of that staff member.
    Password, picture and username are left out on purpose.
  '''
}

Table dim_store {
  store_key int [pk]
  store_id int
  store_name text [note: 'store_id and city']
  manager_staff_id int
  manager_name text [note: 'staff_first_name and staff_last_name']
  address text
  district text
  city text
  postal_code text
  country text
  phone text
  valid_from date
  valid_to date
  is_current boolean
  Note: '''
    500 rows on the first load, built from store. Inventory, and so every rental, only uses 2 of the 500 stores, confirmed by query on the database.
    store_name is store_id plus city, because 126 city names are shared by more than one store, confirmed by query on the database.
    The manager comes from store.manager_staff_id. A change of manager is detected by manager_staff_id, not by the name.
    Type 2: manager (ID and name), so store results can be compared under different managers.
    Type 1: phone and every other column. A Type 1 change overwrites every version of that store. A blank phone is shown as Not specified.
  '''
}

Table dim_film {
  film_key int [pk]
  film_id int
  title text
  description text
  release_year int
  language text
  original_language text
  length int
  rating text
  rental_duration int
  rental_rate numeric(5,2)
  replacement_cost numeric(5,2)
  valid_from date
  valid_to date
  is_current boolean
  Note: '''
    1,000 rows on the first load. Category lives in the bridge table, and actors are left out because a film has many.
    Empty original_language is shown as Not specified.
    Type 2: rental_rate, because price history feeds revenue trend analysis.
    Type 1: replacement_cost and every other column. No report in the model needs an old replacement cost. A Type 1 change overwrites every version of that film.
  '''
}

Table dim_customer {
  customer_key int [pk]
  customer_id int
  email text
  first_name text
  last_name text
  phone text
  country text
  city text
  district text
  address text
  postal_code text
  customer_since date
  is_active boolean
  valid_from date
  valid_to date
  is_current boolean
  Note: '''
    999 rows on the first load, built from customer outward. 2 customers have never rented, and they still belong here.
    Type 2: address, city, district, country and is_active. Geographic reports must credit a rental to where the customer lived at the time, and the dates a customer became active or inactive are analysed.
    Type 1: phone, email and names. A Type 1 change overwrites every version of that customer.
    Type 0: customer_since never changes.
    The uuid column in the source is left out on purpose.
  '''
}

Ref: fact_rental.customer_key > dim_customer.customer_key
Ref: fact_rental.film_key > dim_film.film_key
Ref: fact_rental.store_key > dim_store.store_key
Ref: fact_rental.staff_key > dim_staff.staff_key
Ref: fact_rental.rental_date_key > dim_date.date_key
Ref: fact_rental.return_date_key > dim_date.date_key
Ref: bridge_film_category.film_key > dim_film.film_key
Ref: bridge_film_category.category_key > dim_category.category_key